1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,730
Saturday, May 17, 1980.

2
00:00:03,980 --> 00:00:09,050
Six miles north of Mount St. Helens, in southwest
Washington, a trailer is parked on

3
00:00:09,100 --> 00:00:11,430
a ridge facing the volcano.

4
00:00:11,630 --> 00:00:16,300
The man outside it is David Johnston, a
30-year-old volcanologist with the U.S.

5
00:00:16,350 --> 00:00:18,380
Geological Survey.

6
00:00:18,430 --> 00:00:21,000
Tonight, he is the one keeping watch.

7
00:00:21,350 --> 00:00:24,700
For two months, the mountain
has been waking up.

8
00:00:24,750 --> 00:00:25,620
Earthquakes.

9
00:00:25,670 --> 00:00:27,007
Steam explosions.

10
00:00:27,057 --> 00:00:29,200
A new crater at the summit.

11
00:00:29,450 --> 00:00:33,460
But what worries the scientists most
is on the north side.

12
00:00:33,770 --> 00:00:36,600
We were measuring the rate of, of, uh,

13
00:00:37,330 --> 00:00:41,940
of northward movement of the, the bulge at
about six feet a day, and, uh, we knew

14
00:00:41,990 --> 00:00:43,140
that wasn't so good.

15
00:00:43,610 --> 00:00:48,920
By that evening, part of the north flank had
been pushed outward by more than 450

16
00:00:48,970 --> 00:00:50,100
feet.

17
00:00:50,450 --> 00:00:55,020
At 8:32 the next morning, Johnston's
voice came over the radio.

18
00:00:57,980 --> 00:01:02,330
suddenly I saw this mushroom cloud go up above
the volcano and climb rapidly up into

19
00:01:02,380 --> 00:01:03,110
the stratosphere.

20
00:01:03,160 --> 00:01:03,710
And

21
00:01:03,760 --> 00:01:04,760
called Dave.

22
00:01:05,320 --> 00:01:06,210
And we couldn't get through.

23
00:01:06,260 --> 00:01:08,250
There was, there was no,
there was no answer.

24
00:01:08,770 --> 00:01:13,560
By the time Swanson's plane reached the volcano,
the north side of Mount St. Helens

25
00:01:13,610 --> 00:01:14,800
was gone.

26
00:01:14,850 --> 00:01:17,013
The eruption had gone sideways...

27
00:01:17,063 --> 00:01:20,540
toward the ridge where Johnston
had been keeping watch.

28
00:01:20,890 --> 00:01:24,240
To understand how, we have to go
back two months...

29
00:01:24,290 --> 00:01:26,100
to the first earthquake.

30
00:01:30,750 --> 00:01:33,260
March 20, 1980.

31
00:01:33,310 --> 00:01:37,780
A little before four in the afternoon,
seismographs at the University of Washington

32
00:01:37,830 --> 00:01:41,880
record a magnitude-4 earthquake
beneath Mount St. Helens.

33
00:01:42,130 --> 00:01:46,080
The volcano has not erupted since 1857.

34
00:01:46,130 --> 00:01:48,640
Now the earthquakes don't stop.

35
00:01:48,690 --> 00:01:53,420
Within a week, about thirty a day are
magnitude three or larger.

36
00:01:53,770 --> 00:01:59,240
On March 27, just after noon, a loud boom
is heard around the mountain.

37
00:01:59,290 --> 00:02:03,540
A steam explosion has blasted a crater
through the ice at the summit.

38
00:02:03,790 --> 00:02:08,780
It is the first eruption in the lower 48 states
since Lassen Peak, more than sixty

39
00:02:08,830 --> 00:02:10,520
years earlier.

40
00:02:10,870 --> 00:02:15,833
The explosions are driven by steam, not by
fresh magma reaching the surface.

41
00:02:15,883 --> 00:02:20,100
But they keep coming, and within a week the
crater is more than a thousand feet

42
00:02:20,150 --> 00:02:21,520
across.

43
00:02:21,770 --> 00:02:26,680
Geologists from the U.S. Geological Survey set
up a base in Vancouver, Washington.

44
00:02:26,730 --> 00:02:30,820
One of them, Dwight Crandell, tells a
reporter what worries him.

45
00:02:37,470 --> 00:02:40,780
Crandell knew this volcano better
than almost anyone.

46
00:02:40,830 --> 00:02:44,900
Two years earlier, he and Donal Mullineaux had
published a hazard report on Mount

47
00:02:44,950 --> 00:02:46,280
St. Helens.

48
00:02:46,530 --> 00:02:51,640
It called it an especially dangerous
volcano, and it made a forecast.

49
00:02:51,690 --> 00:02:55,173
An eruption was more likely within
the next hundred years...

50
00:02:55,223 --> 00:02:58,860
and perhaps even before the end
of this century.

51
00:02:59,210 --> 00:03:03,540
The same page is just as clear about
what could not be known.

52
00:03:08,090 --> 00:03:09,840
The danger was known.

53
00:03:09,890 --> 00:03:12,227
The form it would take was not.

54
00:03:12,277 --> 00:03:15,080
And this spring, the clue was
not at the summit.

55
00:03:15,130 --> 00:03:17,220
It was on the north side.

56
00:03:17,630 --> 00:03:20,120
All the deformation on the mountain

57
00:03:20,830 --> 00:03:25,200
was, was local to the mountain, and even more
local than that was on the north side

58
00:03:25,250 --> 00:03:26,250
of the mountain only.

59
00:03:26,670 --> 00:03:30,860
From the first day of the eruption, observers
had noticed an uplift high on the

60
00:03:30,910 --> 00:03:32,140
north flank.

61
00:03:32,190 --> 00:03:35,240
By mid-April, it was impossible to miss.

62
00:03:35,470 --> 00:03:39,220
In the nearly two-month period before May 18,
what was essentially happening at

63
00:03:39,270 --> 00:03:43,780
Mount St. Helens was that magma or molten
material was moving up from some deep

64
00:03:43,830 --> 00:03:49,500
reservoir beneath the mountain up into the
volcano itself and it began to grow, um,

65
00:03:49,550 --> 00:03:52,920
or form what we call a dome or a
cryptodome inside the volcano.

66
00:03:53,190 --> 00:03:58,400
And that inflating body of magma or molten
material actually broke the north side of

67
00:03:58,450 --> 00:04:03,220
the volcano and began to cause the north side
of the volcano to expand out toward

68
00:04:03,270 --> 00:04:04,270
the north.

69
00:04:04,390 --> 00:04:08,820
To follow it, the scientists put survey targets
on the bulge and measured them every

70
00:04:08,870 --> 00:04:14,060
few days: angles by theodolite, distances by
laser, from stations on the ridges to

71
00:04:14,110 --> 00:04:15,220
the north.

72
00:04:15,270 --> 00:04:18,240
One of those stations was Coldwater II.

73
00:04:18,490 --> 00:04:22,200
The measurements told the same
story day after day.

74
00:04:22,250 --> 00:04:27,860
The north flank was moving outward about five
feet a day, at an almost steady rate.

75
00:04:28,110 --> 00:04:32,900
A landslide from that slope was recognized as a
real hazard, one that could set off

76
00:04:32,950 --> 00:04:34,220
an eruption.

77
00:04:34,270 --> 00:04:38,740
What the scientists did not foresee, as they
later wrote, was the scale of what

78
00:04:38,790 --> 00:04:40,200
would follow.

79
00:04:40,750 --> 00:04:43,900
There were also decisions to
make about people.

80
00:04:43,950 --> 00:04:49,020
On April 30, Governor Dixy Lee Ray closed a Red
Zone around the peak, three to eight

81
00:04:49,070 --> 00:04:53,700
miles out, and a wider Blue Zone, where
loggers and landowners could enter with

82
00:04:53,750 --> 00:04:55,640
permits during the day.

83
00:04:55,890 --> 00:04:57,600
Not everyone left.

84
00:04:57,650 --> 00:05:03,080
At Spirit Lake, five miles north of the summit,
83-year-old Harry Truman refused to

85
00:05:03,130 --> 00:05:06,540
leave the lodge he had run for
more than fifty years.

86
00:05:06,790 --> 00:05:09,780
Cabin owners pressed to get
their belongings out.

87
00:05:09,830 --> 00:05:14,980
On Saturday, May 17, deputies escorted about
fifty carloads of them into the Red

88
00:05:15,030 --> 00:05:15,913
Zone.

89
00:05:15,963 --> 00:05:19,680
A second caravan was set for ten
o'clock the next morning.

90
00:05:20,230 --> 00:05:24,660
That afternoon, David Johnston took over the
post at Coldwater II from fellow

91
00:05:24,710 --> 00:05:26,740
geologist Harry Glicken.

92
00:05:26,790 --> 00:05:30,000
It stood 5.7 miles from the summit.

93
00:05:30,250 --> 00:05:35,560
Eight miles northwest, at Coldwater I,
photographer Reid Blackburn of The Columbian

94
00:05:35,610 --> 00:05:38,360
stayed overnight beside a camera station.

95
00:05:38,410 --> 00:05:43,000
On the next ridge north of Johnston, a volunteer
radio operator, Gerry Martin, was

96
00:05:43,050 --> 00:05:44,900
also keeping watch.

97
00:05:45,250 --> 00:05:50,640
At 6:53 on Sunday morning, Johnston radioed
in his last survey readings.

98
00:05:50,690 --> 00:05:53,100
Nothing in the data looked unusual.

99
00:05:53,450 --> 00:06:00,260
At 8:32:11, a magnitude 5.1 earthquake
struck beneath the volcano.

100
00:06:00,510 --> 00:06:04,780
Johnston's call to Vancouver was picked up
on a private citizen's radio.

101
00:06:04,830 --> 00:06:07,740
A second message was too
garbled to understand.

102
00:06:07,790 --> 00:06:09,720
Then, nothing.

103
00:06:10,270 --> 00:06:14,740
What happened in the next minutes has been
reconstructed in detail, from photographs

104
00:06:14,790 --> 00:06:18,920
taken by survivors, and from what
the eruption left behind.

105
00:06:19,170 --> 00:06:23,400
Within about twenty seconds of the earthquake,
the bulge and the summit above it

106
00:06:23,450 --> 00:06:28,220
broke loose and slid north, in a
series of enormous blocks.

107
00:06:28,470 --> 00:06:33,940
It was the largest landslide in recorded
history: about two-thirds of a cubic mile

108
00:06:33,990 --> 00:06:35,360
of rock.

109
00:06:35,610 --> 00:06:39,960
And as it moved, it stripped away the rock that
had been holding in the magma inside

110
00:06:40,010 --> 00:06:41,540
the north flank.

111
00:06:41,730 --> 00:06:45,000
On the morning of May 18, what actually
happened, a landslide basically uncorked

112
00:06:46,050 --> 00:06:51,160
this pressurized body of magma and allowed it
to, um, explode or expand out towards

113
00:06:51,210 --> 00:06:52,739
the north very rapidly.

114
00:06:52,869 --> 00:06:54,960
This is what we call the lateral blast.

115
00:06:55,390 --> 00:06:59,380
The blast tore out of the side of the
mountain toward the north, and quickly

116
00:06:59,430 --> 00:07:01,100
overtook the avalanche.

117
00:07:01,150 --> 00:07:05,240
Within about six miles, almost
nothing was left standing.

118
00:07:05,490 --> 00:07:10,720
Farther out, entire forests were flattened, the
trees all pointing the way the blast

119
00:07:10,770 --> 00:07:11,973
had moved.

120
00:07:12,023 --> 00:07:16,620
At its outer edge, the trees stayed
upright, but were scorched dead.

121
00:07:16,870 --> 00:07:19,940
The directed blast was really the most
destructive event that occurred on the

122
00:07:19,990 --> 00:07:21,240
morning of May 18.

123
00:07:21,650 --> 00:07:26,680
It completely destroyed an area of 230 square
miles in the matter of, um, somewhere

124
00:07:26,730 --> 00:07:29,200
between five and, and nine minutes.

125
00:07:29,670 --> 00:07:32,660
Coldwater II was inside that area.

126
00:07:32,710 --> 00:07:34,740
So was Gerry Martin's ridge.

127
00:07:34,790 --> 00:07:39,060
His last words on the radio described the blast
covering the camper and the car to

128
00:07:39,110 --> 00:07:40,260
the south of him...

129
00:07:40,310 --> 00:07:42,240
and coming toward him.

130
00:07:42,690 --> 00:07:45,040
Then the eruption turned upward.

131
00:07:45,090 --> 00:07:50,480
Within fifteen minutes, a column of ash rose
about fifteen miles into the sky.

132
00:07:50,530 --> 00:07:53,660
It kept erupting for nine hours.

133
00:07:54,010 --> 00:07:58,013
When it ended, the summit was
1,300 feet lower.

134
00:07:58,063 --> 00:08:01,133
In its place was a crater,
open to the north...

135
00:08:01,183 --> 00:08:04,580
the direction the mountain had
been bulging for weeks.

136
00:08:04,930 --> 00:08:09,280
The scientists later wrote that the landslide
and the blast had greatly exceeded

137
00:08:09,330 --> 00:08:10,800
their expectations.

138
00:08:10,850 --> 00:08:13,333
The bulge had been measured every day.

139
00:08:13,383 --> 00:08:16,940
What it would do when it failed
had not been foreseen.

140
00:08:17,490 --> 00:08:22,080
The avalanche buried the upper valley of the
North Fork Toutle River for fourteen

141
00:08:22,130 --> 00:08:23,420
miles.

142
00:08:23,670 --> 00:08:28,900
Water from melting snow and ice, and from the
avalanche itself, became mudflows that

143
00:08:28,950 --> 00:08:31,860
raced down the river valleys for
the rest of the day.

144
00:08:31,910 --> 00:08:36,500
They destroyed bridges, and reached the Cowlitz
River, about fifty miles downstream,

145
00:08:36,550 --> 00:08:38,160
around midnight.

146
00:08:38,410 --> 00:08:40,520
The ash drifted east.

147
00:08:40,570 --> 00:08:44,280
In Yakima, by mid-morning, it
was as dark as night.

148
00:08:44,330 --> 00:08:50,180
In Spokane, 250 miles away, day turned
into complete darkness.

149
00:08:50,830 --> 00:08:53,020
57 people died.

150
00:08:53,070 --> 00:08:56,280
Most of them were outside the
restricted zones.

151
00:08:56,630 --> 00:08:59,540
David Johnston's body was never found.

152
00:08:59,590 --> 00:09:05,513
Reid Blackburn was found in his car at Coldwater
I, buried in ash up to the windows.

153
00:09:05,563 --> 00:09:09,840
Harry Truman and his lodge lie under the
avalanche at Spirit Lake.

154
00:09:10,290 --> 00:09:14,420
I remember thinking up in the airplane that it,
Dave just couldn't have survived

155
00:09:14,850 --> 00:09:18,640
this, um, especially when we got around to the
west side and saw all this ash headed

156
00:09:18,690 --> 00:09:19,880
in his direction.

157
00:09:20,390 --> 00:09:24,820
For those who lived, so much depended on
where they happened to be.

158
00:09:24,870 --> 00:09:28,820
Mike and Lu Moore were camping with their two
small daughters about thirteen miles

159
00:09:28,870 --> 00:09:29,807
north.

160
00:09:29,857 --> 00:09:33,300
They sheltered in a hunter's cabin, then
walked out through the ash until a

161
00:09:33,350 --> 00:09:35,520
helicopter lifted them out.

162
00:09:35,770 --> 00:09:40,120
Jim Scymanky was working with a logging crew
about twelve miles northwest when the

163
00:09:40,170 --> 00:09:42,253
blast cloud overran them.

164
00:09:42,303 --> 00:09:46,360
Of the four men burned there, he was
the only one who survived.

165
00:09:46,410 --> 00:09:49,060
National Guard helicopters got him out.

166
00:09:49,310 --> 00:09:55,100
By dusk on May 18, military and civilian
helicopter crews had rescued or evacuated

167
00:09:55,150 --> 00:09:57,660
more than 130 people.

168
00:09:57,710 --> 00:10:01,767
Air Force crews alone were credited with 61
rescues from life-threatening

169
00:10:01,817 --> 00:10:03,320
situations.

170
00:10:03,570 --> 00:10:08,560
In the days that followed, the search moved into
the blast zone, for survivors, and

171
00:10:08,610 --> 00:10:10,460
then for the missing.

172
00:10:10,890 --> 00:10:15,720
So I guess I had the realization right away
that, um, this was some kind of a

173
00:10:16,030 --> 00:10:20,320
tragedy and, uh, on the one hand it was this
huge, exciting, and interesting

174
00:10:20,370 --> 00:10:22,740
magmatic eruption and on the
other hand it was...

175
00:10:23,170 --> 00:10:25,680
I was pretty certain that something
terrible had happened to Dave.

176
00:10:25,730 --> 00:10:28,880
So it was a, it was a, a
strange day for me.

177
00:10:29,510 --> 00:10:32,800
Within weeks, the scientists went back in.

178
00:10:32,910 --> 00:10:38,420
Before the dust had literally settled in the
summer of 1980, um, there were USGS

179
00:10:38,470 --> 00:10:43,380
scientists swarming all over the area out in the
blast zone studying the pyroclastic

180
00:10:43,430 --> 00:10:48,340
flows, studying the debris avalanche deposit,
studying the directed blast deposit.

181
00:10:48,650 --> 00:10:53,800
They found Spirit Lake about 200 feet higher
than before, its surface covered with

182
00:10:53,850 --> 00:10:56,600
thousands of logs from the
forest around it.

183
00:10:56,650 --> 00:11:01,300
Where water reached the hot deposits, they
were still exploding with steam.

184
00:11:01,650 --> 00:11:05,440
Mount St. Helens erupted again five
more times that year.

185
00:11:05,490 --> 00:11:09,100
This time, the scientists were
watching for the signs.

186
00:11:09,270 --> 00:11:13,280
The subsequent eruptions were actually, most of
them were forecast fairly accurately

187
00:11:13,330 --> 00:11:15,340
by the USGS team of scientists.

188
00:11:15,770 --> 00:11:19,760
So when it looked like another explosion was
about to take place, our helicopter

189
00:11:19,810 --> 00:11:24,220
crews would pick us up and we'd move out to the
outskirts of the, of the blast zone.

190
00:11:24,650 --> 00:11:29,800
In 1982, the Survey's base in Vancouver was
dedicated as the Cascades Volcano

191
00:11:29,850 --> 00:11:32,980
Observatory, in David Johnston's memory.

192
00:11:33,230 --> 00:11:38,920
Four years later, after mudflows from a volcano
in Colombia killed more than 20,000

193
00:11:39,110 --> 00:11:44,280
people, the Survey created a team to help other
countries watch their volcanoes.

194
00:11:44,530 --> 00:11:50,320
In 1991, that team and Philippine scientists
warned that Mount Pinatubo was about to

195
00:11:50,370 --> 00:11:51,360
erupt.

196
00:11:51,410 --> 00:11:54,267
Tens of thousands of people
were moved out.

197
00:11:54,317 --> 00:11:59,020
The forecasts are estimated to have
saved at least 5,000 lives.

198
00:11:59,370 --> 00:12:05,320
we learned a lot about how you interact with the
civil defense, with the public, um,

199
00:12:05,370 --> 00:12:11,360
with the press, and that was transferred by
the press to the world, and as

200
00:12:11,410 --> 00:12:16,700
a result, I think volcanology took a
quantum leap in science as well as

201
00:12:16,750 --> 00:12:19,480
applicability to, to society's needs.

202
00:12:20,070 --> 00:12:24,500
The ridge where David Johnston kept watch
is now called Johnston Ridge.

203
00:12:24,550 --> 00:12:28,380
From the observatory there, you look straight
into the crater that opened to the

204
00:12:28,430 --> 00:12:31,760
north on the morning of May 18, 1980.
